From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: sun8i-ce - Add support for the A733

The A733 crypto engine keeps the registers and algorithm codes of the
earlier engines but takes other task descriptors (A733 manual, section
19.1): symmetric and hash/RNG tasks have their own layouts, addresses
are 40-bit byte addresses in five bytes and the scatter-gather lengths
are in bytes (19.1.4.14, 19.1.4.15). Each channel feeds one algorithm
unit and has its own load bit in CE_TLR, CE_ISR holds a done and a fail
status and CE_ESR an error code per channel, and there is no CE_CTR
(19.1.7).

Add a variant flag for this interface. The two layouts share the
descriptor memory with struct ce_task in a union; the first four words
of the symmetric layout are those of struct ce_task. Ciphers run on
channel 0, hashes and the TRNG on channel 2, the hash unit. The hash
keeps the padding by the driver (19.1.4.9), gives its length in bits
(the manual names no unit; with bytes or words the engine reports a data
length error) and leaves the last package flag clear, so the CE writes
the state after the padded message, which is the digest. The TRNG task
selects SHA256 as its post-processing, as the vendor driver does: the
manual says the output is post-processed with SHA256 (19.1.1) but names
no value for the field. Its length is given in bytes; the engine does
not check it, any unit gave random data on the chip. The module clock
runs at 300 MHz, in the range of the CE clock domain (19.1.2). Other
SoCs are unchanged.

Not covered: a 40-bit DMA mask (buffers above 4 GiB are bounced), the
byte alignment the A733 accepts, and the further modes and units.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: the ciphers and hashes pass the crypto
self-tests on the engine, and the TRNG gives random data.

Assisted-by: LLM
